<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n25466" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">labdăcismus</span></b> , i,
		m., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">λαβδακισμός</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>labdacism</i>, a
			fault in speaking. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>The too frequent use of the letter l</i> (as, sol
			et luna luce lucebant alba, levi, lactea), <i><span class="en">Mart.
				Cap. 5, § 514</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>An erroneous pronunciation of the letter l</i>,
			like the Spanish ll, <i><span class="en">Diom. 448</span></i> P.;
					<i><span class="en">Isid. Orig. 1, 31, 8</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>